We know this patch, the problem for a normal debian user is:
How to patch glibc and compile it the debian way. When downloading 
the source package there is no clear possibility to patch it and then 
run dpkg-buildpackage. The problem is that the make script unzip 
linuxthreads and copy directory trees around. Any change in the 
source is made obsolete by dpkg-buildpackage.

So my question: HOW TO PATCH & COMPILE glibc from debian source 
package with dpkg-buildpackage?

At this time I am running an older glibc deb package (set to HOLD, 
from October last year) with a replaced libpthreads.so. Now I have 
the problems that the list of hold packages gets longer and longer 
because some newer libs depend on newer versions of libc. The 
replaced version of libpthreads.so was compiled by Tom Evans.

